why: 1. we are out of storage - storage is full. 2. we have w /r throughput bottleneck (especially write cuz read can scale pretty well with replicas - we need more databases to handle independently data. whne making multiple writes or reads we always hurt the other one. 

partitiong - have the data stored different databases distributed by some technic. then each DB can scale on its own. 
tehcnics:
1. just by some order of column - for example shard by names. A-D on db A etc..
pros- good for range queries - similar names will be prob in same shard so no need mix shards in 1 call 
cons- hot spots - there might be many names start with A but not many with Z, then data isn't distributed evenly, which will cause waste of resources on the other hand for Z shard, and bottleneck in A shard - whcih will eventually cuz it to shard itself. 

2. hash by unique colum (id for example). hash will distribute evenly the column we chose. 
pros- no hot spot on that column
cons- it won't gurantee distrubte evenly. for example - we make shards by postId. famous actor will have so many likes and comments that will get this shard comapre to when i post. still can have hotspots. and range queries aren't fast since ranged values arent prob in same shard. 



what can help - secondary index. 
we can index each shard based on some value. for example by height. then inside the shard it will be easy to query by height. 
still for range queries for example get all 6.3 height - we need to go through all shards. 

for that we have global secondary index.
that means besides the hash sharding we have - we will make new shards that will be stored by different distribution. for example below 6.3 and above 6.3. 
pros - easy to range queries
cons- need to write for 2 different shards every time. makes w slow. and complication ofcourse what if one fails ... . .  . 
